Two related questions:
Is there a way to do this without ggstances? For example, using coord_flip()?
Something with this skelton is what I am thinking:

ggplot(melt_simple,aes(x=OTU,y=Abundance))+
  geom_boxplot(data= melt_simple,position=position_dodge(),size=.3,alpha=0.5) +
  scale_color_manual() + scale_fill_manual() +  coord_flip() + scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0,300))

Another issue I am having is adding color to a plot like this. Since there are multiple Samples for OTUs/a single tree tip, I assumed that providing to the mapping argument color=Sample would work, but it does not. How would colors be mapped to represent this? The problemeatic lines of code are in red below, for a facet_plot example and for a simple ggplot geom_boxplot object (that one could, e.g., use facet_grid to match with a tree).

​facet_plot(g.left, panel="Boxplot", data=melt_simple, geom_boxploth, mapping = aes(x=Abundance,group=label,color=Sample), outlier.shape=NA) + ylim(0, 135)
ggplot(melt_simple,aes(x=OTU,y=Abundance))+
  geom_boxplot(data= melt_simple,position=position_dodge(),aes(colour=Sample),size=.3,alpha=0.5) +
  scale_color_manual() + scale_fill_manual() +  coord_flip() + scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0,300))

