Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Is it possible to mimic the behaviour of figtree where the colour of the branches is determined based on the status of the node they connect to and then a gradient applied E.g.

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-VYfZnS_sFmI/WNAN6aSIUwI/AAAAAAAABUk/HaX-Hehi37EQaoMLCsuAm_Ux99hbpLimwCLcB/s1600/Screen%2BShot%2B2017-03-20%2Bat%2B17.14.02.png>

Rather than this -

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-a3bPB7VSMF4/WNAOPUP91bI/AAAAAAAABUo/aBvTqs5iAYgseJzd8Sq5Mg0W-7X94PWQQCLcB/s1600/Screen%2BShot%2B2017-03-20%2Bat%2B17.15.19.png>

In FigTree this is done with the Gradient check box under Appearance.

I can achieve the appearance without the gradient in ggtree, e.g. where location is a discrete trait and location.prob the posterior probability of that trait:

pal = brewer.pal(7,"Set1")

ggtree(beast_tree, mrsd=df$dt) +

   geom_tree(aes(color=as.factor(location), size=location.prob)) +

   theme_tree2() +

   scale_color_manual(values = pal) +

   scale_size("line", range=c(0, 1.2), guide=FALSE)

Is it possible to adapt this code to look like the former?

Thanks

